WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.500
The linter checks style, not whether the output is valid JSON.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.050
A contract test checks the format. A regression test checks that the old mode still works.

3
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:22.050
Diff review asks another question: what actually changed, and was it permitted?

4
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:29.550
Tests pass on revision A and produce a receipt.

5
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:37.100
A later edit creates B. The receipt remains valid history, but it does not prove B.

6
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:44.100
Delivery needs evidence bound to the current candidate and its environment.

7
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:51.600
The test fails because it detects that the previous output disappeared.

8
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.600
Deleting that assertion produces green without fixing the behaviour.

9
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:06.900
Changes to the verifier also need review. Passing a weakened test does not satisfy the contract.

10
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:14.400
With no option, report keeps its text output.

11
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.400
With --json, it returns an object that can be parsed and validated.

12
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:29.700
Test both paths and invalid input. Checking only the new mode leaves a regression invisible.

13
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:37.200
The run has a budget to prevent unbounded work.

14
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.200
The counter reaches zero while a mandatory check is still pending.

15
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:51.750
The correct status is incomplete, with the available evidence. A timeout does not become acceptance.

16
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:59.250
In this illustrative example, 16 of 20 runs satisfy the contract.

17
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:06.250
The other four fail for different reasons that must remain in the record.

18
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:13.800
That 80% describes this sample and environment. It is not a universal model guarantee.

